  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' covers the index `kibana_sample_data_logs`. Its time field is `timestamp`. The fields include `response` (text, with a `response.keyword` subfield), `bytes`, `clientip`, `extension`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `request`, `url`, `utc_time` and others.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-d296f048 Web logs' (id `a1ca35a2-306f-423e-8831-58479caa37a4`) has two panels. One is a line chart of request count over time. The other is a bar chart of request count by `response.keyword`.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-d296f048 5xx errors' (id `917fef34-3a06-4e9b-96f8-f47b94a49d0e`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 documents match in the last 5 minutes. A document matches when `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\". Because the codes are all three digits, that range covers every 5xx code. I created it with `enabled: true`, so it didn't need enabling afterwards.\n4. **Panel edit:** I renamed the response-code panel to 'Responses by code', and the update response shows the new title. The read-back of the rule shows `enabled: true`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
